\subsection{$N$-Sensitivity}
\label{sec:n_sensitivity}

The significance ASV exhibits a sharp phase transition: for mediation at $\tau{=}0.05$, ASV-filtered power is exactly 0 for $N{\leq}5{,}000$ (blocking all rejections), then jumps to 1.0 at $N{=}10{,}000$. The critical sample size $N^* \approx 6{,}000$--$10{,}000$ is analytically computable, providing practitioners with a concrete minimum sample size for their topology and effect size.

\subsection{Binarization Collapse}
\label{sec:binarization}

Collapsing $K{=}3$ annotations to binary introduces additional bias varying 147$\times$ across topologies: from $\bar{\Delta}{=}0.006$ (M-bias) to $\bar{\Delta}{=}0.926$ (IV). Exposure and IV also introduce sign flips during binarization (14\% and 17.5\%), a phenomenon absent in the other topologies. Full results are in Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary} (Figure~\ref{fig:binarization}).

\subsection{Empirical Validation}
\label{sec:empirical}

We validate the theoretical framework using real LLM confusion matrices estimated from HumAID~\cite{alam2021humaid}, a 10-class disaster response classification task ($K{=}10$). Three frontier LLMs (Llama-4-Maverick, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-Chat) annotate HumAID samples under zero-shot and few-shot prompting, yielding 8 usable model$\times$prompt configurations (one configuration excluded due to API failure). Each configuration produces a $10{\times}10$ confusion matrix $C$, which we plug into the analytical bias expressions from Section~\ref{sec:method} for all seven topologies.

\paragraph{Theory--practice alignment.}
The empirical bias ranking across topologies matches the theoretical predictions from the random-matrix analysis. IV produces the largest bias (12.4--42.1\% relative bias across configurations), followed by exposure (9.0--17.4\%), mediation and front-door (10.7--14.8\% each), while collider (4.1--5.6\%), confounding (4.9--6.2\%), and M-bias (5.1--6.5\%) remain below 10\%. This ordering is consistent with the ASV vulnerability spectrum from Section~\ref{sec:asv_results}: IV is maximally fragile and collider is near-immune. Zero sign reversals occur across all 56 topology$\times$configuration combinations ($8 \times 7$), consistent with the diagonal dominance of all estimated confusion matrices (mean diagonal 0.671--0.724).

\paragraph{ASV diagnostic results.}
Of 63 scenario-level ASV evaluations ($9 \times 7$; the failed configuration contributes 7 trivially-safe entries), 49.2\% (31/63) are flagged as ASV-unsafe, indicating that roughly half of LLM annotation scenarios require correction or sensitivity reporting. Magnitude ASV values are triggered for four topologies: IV (0.31--0.83), mediation (0.62--0.90), front-door (0.62--0.91), and exposure (0.65--1.11). The remaining three topologies (confounding, collider, M-bias) produce bias below the 10\% threshold across all configurations, requiring no ASV report (Figure~\ref{fig:asv_heatmap}). Sign-flip ASV is undefined for all configurations, consistent with the zero observed sign reversals at $K{=}10$ with diagonal-dominant $C$.

\paragraph{Confusion matrix structure.}
Macro $F_1$ ranges from 0.614 to 0.690 across models, but $F_1$ does not predict downstream causal bias: within this narrow range, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude---from 4.1\% (collider) to 42.1\% (IV)---demonstrating that topology, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk (Figure~\ref{fig:f1_vs_bias}). Inter-model error correlations are low (0.051--0.157), favorable for multi-annotator correction with model-diverse gold-label subsets.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_f1_vs_bias.pdf}
\caption{Topology determines downstream causal bias, not F1. Each point represents one LLM annotator $\times$ topology combination from HumAID ($K{=}10$, 8 configurations $\times$ 7 topologies $=$ 56 points). Within a narrow $F_1$ range (0.614--0.690), relative bias varies $10{\times}$: IV reaches 42.1\% while collider stays below 5.6\%.}
\label{fig:f1_vs_bias}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Correction Guidance}
\label{sec:correction}

We evaluate five correction methods across all seven topologies under six error structures (210 configurations total): naive plug-in, maximum likelihood adjustment (MLA), design-based supervised learning (DSL)~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023}, MC-SIMEX, and prediction-powered inference (PPI++)~\cite{angelopoulos2023}. Full per-topology results are in Table~\ref{tab:correction} and the decision procedure in Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction} (Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{FWL invariance.}
For four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias), the Frisch--Waugh--Lovell theorem implies that applying $C^{-1}$ to the dummy variables cannot change the $T$ coefficient. Naive plug-in and MLA achieve exactly 0.0\% bias reduction across these four topologies.

\paragraph{Method ranking.}
PPI++ achieves the highest grand-mean bias reduction (93.8\%) with nominal coverage (96.8\%), but requires gold-standard labels. MC-SIMEX provides moderate gold-free correction (20.9\%), serving as the only viable fallback for IV (64.7\% reduction). DSL corrects confounding (49.8\%) and mediation (70.3\%) but catastrophically amplifies bias for M-bias ($-$768\%). No single method dominates: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the \emph{structure} of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference.

The consequences can be catastrophic. Under $K{=}3$ non-differential exposure misclassification, 83.4\% of confusion matrices cause \emph{sign reversal} of the estimated treatment effect, where the estimated direction of the causal relationship is opposite to the truth. This failure breaks the classical binary guarantee that non-differential misclassification biases estimates ``toward the null''~\cite{brenner1993}, a guarantee that many applied researchers implicitly rely upon. The breakdown is not an edge case: it persists even when classifier accuracy exceeds 70\% (13.7\% violation rate at diagonal $\geq 0.7$). Moreover, standard classification metrics do not predict downstream causal risk: within a narrow $F_1$ range of 0.614--0.690 across three frontier LLMs, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies (from 4.1\% to 42.1\%), illustrating that \emph{topology}, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk.

The mechanism is algebraic. For binary classification, non-differential error can only attenuate the effect because the $2{\times}2$ stochastic confusion matrix preserves coefficient signs. With $K \geq 3$ categories, the confusion matrix can assign negative effective weights to categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{brenner1993} identified the possibility of polytomous reversal but did not characterize it across causal designs. \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology (unobserved confounding). No existing framework addresses how the same confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across different causal identification strategies.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification (closed-form for confounding, exposure, and mediation; numerically validated for all seven). We establish that five topologies maintain a structural sign-flip guarantee (with formal proofs for three topologies and Monte Carlo validation for all seven), while exposure and IV topologies can produce sign reversal. This taxonomy reveals that bias direction is determined by the \emph{interaction} between the confusion matrix and the DAG topology, not by either alone. The taxonomy extends to binarization: collapsing $K{=}3$ to binary introduces additional bias varying up to 147$\times$ across topologies.

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs).} We propose a unified sensitivity analysis framework with three complementary measures: magnitude ASV (E-value analogue for annotation error), significance ASV (hypothesis test filter for attenuating topologies), and sign-flip ASV (for exposure and IV). ASVs reveal a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile, 52.6\% sign reversal; sign-flip ASV $= 1.1$--$6.7$) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$), providing practitioners with topology-aware robustness diagnostics.

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction guidance.} We evaluate five correction methods across all seven topologies and find a negative result with significant practical implications that, while theoretically predicted by Frisch--Waugh--Lovell, has been overlooked in the annotation correction literature: matrix-based corrections (naive plug-in, MLA) achieve exactly 0\% bias reduction for four of seven topologies, because the column space of the design matrix is invariant to invertible confusion-matrix transformations. Among gold-free methods, only MC-SIMEX provides positive bias reduction (20.9\% grand mean). PPI++ achieves 93.8\% bias reduction with nominal coverage but requires gold-standard labels.
\end{enumerate}

